      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town, originally called Toll Gate, was renamed Hamilton in honor of Captain Albert James Hamilton, a state legislator who donated forty acres for the courthouse.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        10,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamilton%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The 1901 sandstone courthouse survived a 1887 fire that destroyed the original wooden building, marking a resilient chapter in Hamilton’s history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamilton%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Hamilton sits in the valley of the Buttahatchee River, a tributary of the Tombigbee, winding through the city’s eastern side.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
